{
  "dom_id": "step-q105435",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{12-4x}+\\sqrt{5x+8}$ is continuous.",
  "worked_solution": "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{12-4x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4x\\ge 0\\,\u27f9\\,4x\\le 12\\,\u27f9\\,x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{5x+8}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+8\\ge 0\\,\u27f9\\,5x\\ge -8\\,\u27f9\\,x\\ge -\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,3]\\cap [-\\frac{8}{5},\\infty )=[-\\frac{8}{5},3],\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval.",
